USB Hardware Testing

👉 "Use RegShot to take a registry snapshot before and after plugging in a USB device to detect changes."

⭐ To identify USB devices connected to your system:
● On Linux: Use the "lsusb" "lsusb -v" command to list all connected USB devices.
● On Windows: Access the Device Manager to view and manage connected USB devices.


⭐ USB Sniffing and Debugging
Analyzing USB traffic can help in debugging and ensuring proper communication between devices:
● Wireshark: A network protocol analyzer that can capture and analyze USB traffic.
● USBlyzer: A Windows-based tool for monitoring USB devices and analyzing their activity.

⭐ Check USB Registry Entries
● Press Win + R, type regedit, and press Enter.
Navigate to USB Registry Paths
    < HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Enum\USB\ >

● USB Storage Status
            < HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\USBSTOR >
●  If Start is set to 4, USB storage is disabled.

● Check USB History
    < HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Enum\USBSTOR >
Here, you'll find details of previously connected USB drives.
Suspicious Devices (BadUSB) may appear here instead of USBSTOR



⭐ Check USB Mount Location
● Check via Disk Management:
● Press Win + R, type diskmgmt.msc, and press Enter.
$ Find the USB drive and note the drive letter.

● Check via Command Prompt (CMD):
Open Command Prompt (cmd).
Run:
    $ wmic logicaldisk get caption, description, filesystem, volumename
This lists all mounted drives and their properties.

⭐ Check USB Device in Device Manager

● Open Device Manager:
● Press Win + X → Select Device Manager.
● Expand "Disk Drives" and "Universal Serial Bus Controllers" or check evry ware you usb was mounted.

# Locate the USB drive.
● Right-click → Properties → Check driver info.

👉 Check USB VID and PID
● Go to Details tab → Select Hardware IDs.
Instance 
VID = Vendor ID
PID = Product ID
Also search on internet this VID & PID and get the information about this hardware

✅ Can Reveal Fake Devices – If a flash drive appears as a keyboard or network device, it’s suspicious.
How to Check in Devices and Printers:
● Open "Devices and Printers" (Control Panel → Devices and Printers).
● Look for the USB Device.
👉 Right-click → Properties and check if the type matches what it should be.

⭐ USB Analysis using USBDeview
● Get detailed information on USB devices.

1. Download USBDeview (Download Link).
2. Run USBDeview as Administrator.
3. Find the connected USB drive and check:
● Device name
● Serial number
● VID & PID
● Power consumption

⭐ Check USB Power Consumption
● Measure how much power the USB device draws.
● Open Command Prompt (cmd) as Administrator.
     <$ powercfg /batteryreport >
● Use USBDeview to check the USB power consumption.


⭐ Performance Test (Read/Write Speed)
1. Download and run CrystalDiskMark (Download).
2. Select the USB drive and start the test.
3. Check read/write speeds.


⭐ USB Forensic Analysis (this tool is paid but also having community edition)
● Check past USB connections.
Steps:
1. Download USBDetective (Download).
2. Run it to analyze historical USB device connections.

⭐ Add Free Alternatives to USBDetective
● USB Historian (Extracts historical USB connection data).
● USBLogView (Monitors USB plug/unplug events).


